===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|archaic}} To feel compassion (for someone or with regard to something); to [[regard]] (someone or something) with compassion.
#: {{syn|en|pity|feel sorry for|Thesaurus:pity}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1602|translator=w:Thomas Lodge|title=The Famous and Memorable Workes of {{w|Flavius Josephus|Josephus}}|location=London|author=G. Bishop; et al|chapter=6|page=733|url=http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A04680.0001.001
|passage={{...}} seeing them die so wofully in the flames, he '''compassionated''' them.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|1|2|6|83|text=The Justice which Mr. ''Allworthy'' had executed on ''Partridge,'' at first met with universal Approbation; but no sooner had he felt its Consequences, than his Neighbours began to relent, and to '''compassionate''' his Case;}}
#* {{RQ:Godwin Caleb Williams|volume=2|chapter=1|page=4|passage=And yet I could not help bitterly '''compassionating''' the honest fellow, brought to the gallows, as he was, strictly speaking, by the machinations of that devil incarnate, Mr. Tyrrel.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1815|author={{w|Edward Forster (writer)|Edward Forster}}|title=w:The Arabian Nights|location=London|publisher=William Miller|volume=IV|page=121|passage=Some even laughed at him; but the more serious, and especially all those who had been on friendly terms, or ever had any business with him, most sincerely '''compassionated''' him.}}
#* {{RQ:Charlotte Bronte Jane Eyre|I|III|38|text=“{{...}} if she were a nice, pretty child, one might '''compassionate''' her forlornness; but one really cannot care for such a little toad as that.”}}
#* {{RQ:Thackeray Pendennis|chapter=16|text=Helen laughed at these sentimental remarks, and wondered that Madame herself did not '''compassionate''' her lodger, and console him}}
#* {{RQ:Douglass Bondage|chapter=17|page=236|passage=I explained the circumstances of the past two days, which had driven me to the woods, and he deeply '''compassionated''' my distress.}}

